1. Set up your project directory: Create a new folder for your Chrome Extension and add the following files: `manifest.json`, `background.js`, and `content_script.js`.

2. Configure `manifest.json`: This file is essential for defining your Chrome Extension's metadata and permissions. Add the following content to `manifest.json`:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "My Chrome Extension",
  "version": "1.0",
  "action": {
    "default_title": "Inject Script"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [{
    "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
    "js": ["content_script.js"]
  }],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  }
}
```
Replace `"My Chrome Extension"` with the name of your extension, and update the description if needed. The `matches` property in the `content_scripts` array allows you to target specific URLs or use the wildcard `<all_urls>` for all websites.

3. Implement your JavaScript code in `content_script.js`: This script will be injected into the webpages. Write the logic for manipulating the webpage here. For instance, you can select elements, modify their content, or make AJAX requests.

