2. Create a dataset of labeled sentences and their corresponding categories as a `List<Tuple<string, string>>`. Replace `sentences` with your actual data.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public static List<Tuple<string, string>> CreateDataset()
{
    return new List<Tuple<string, string>>
    {
        Tuple.Create("The sports game was exciting.", "sports"),
        Tuple.Create("The political speech was inspiring.", "politics"),
        Tuple.Create("The movie was hilarious.", "entertainment"),
        // Add more data here...
    };
}
```

3. Transform the dataset into a document-term matrix using the `CountVectorizer` class.

```csharp
using Accord.Statistics.Text.Transforms;
using Accord.Numerics;

public static Matrix Vectorize(IEnumerable<Tuple<string, string>> dataset)
{
    var corpus = dataset.Select(x => x.Item1).ToArray();
    var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer(stopWords: new HashSet<string>(StopWordsEnglish.Instances.Words), maxDocumentFrequency: 0.9);
    return vectorizer.FitTransform(corpus);
}
```

4. Fit the Naive Bayes classifier to the document-term matrix.

```csharp
public static NaiveBayes FitNaiveBayes(Matrix X, IList<string> categories)
{
    var nb = new NaiveBayes();
    nb.Learn(X.Rows, categories);
    return nb;
}
