 I'm here to help you write a React utility function to remove widows from text in your components. Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is left-aligned and only consists of one or a few words, which can sometimes look unsightly. Here's a simple utility function using the `react-document-parser` library to parse and manipulate the DOM nodes:

1. First, install the required libraries by running `npm install react-document-parser react-dom` in your project.

2. Now, create a new utility file named `removeWidows.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { Document, Element, Text } from 'react-document-parser';

const removeWidows = (text, lineHeight) => {
  const lines = text.split('\n');
  let currentLineWidth = 0;
  const linesWithNoWidows = [];

  for (const line of lines) {
    if (!line || line === '\n') continue;

    const parsedDocument = (
      <Document>
        <Element tagName="p" style={{ whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap', lineHeight: `${lineHeight}px` }}>
          <Text text={line} />
        </Element>
      </Document>
    );

    const width = document.createElement('div').appendChild(ReactDOMServer.renderToString(parsedDocument)).clientWidth;
    currentLineWidth += width;

    linesWithNoWidows.push(line);

    if ((currentLineWidth > 0 && line.length > 3) && (width < document.documentElement.clientWidth - 30)) {
      linesWithNoWidows.pop();
      break;
    }
  }

  return linesWithNoWidows.join('\n');
};

export default removeWidows;
```

This function takes two arguments: `text` (a string containing the text to be processed), and `lineHeight` (the height of each line in pixels). It splits the input text into lines, then calculates the width of each line using `react-document-parser`. If it detects a widow, it removes the previous line and breaks the loop.

